message.content:
Below is a minimal, self‑contained example that shows how to take a **substring** of a `char[]` (starting at index 5, length 10) and write it to an XML text stream while letting the XML serializer take care of escaping special characters (`<`, `>`, `&`, etc.).  
The example uses **StAX** (`XMLStreamWriter`) because it gives you fine‑grained control over the exact characters you want to emit, yet it still performs the necessary XML escaping for you.

```java
import javax.xml.stream.XMLOutputFactory;
import javax.xml.stream.XMLStreamException;
import javax.xml.stream.XMLStreamWriter;
import java.io.StringWriter;

public class XmlAppendExample {

    public static void main(String[] args) throws XMLStreamException {
        // 1. Your source character buffer
        char[] textBuffer = "Hello, this is a sample text buffer that contains many characters."
                .toCharArray();

        // 2. Create a StringWriter (or any other Writer) that will hold the XML output
        StringWriter stringWriter = new StringWriter();

        // 3. Create an XMLStreamWriter that writes to the StringWriter
        XMLStreamWriter xmlWriter = XMLOutputFactory.newInstance()
                .createXMLStreamWriter(stringWriter);

        // 4. Start the XML document
        xmlWriter.writeStartDocument("UTF-8", "1.0");
        xmlWriter.writeStartElement("root");          // <root>
        xmlWriter.writeStartElement("content");       //   <content>

        // 5. Append the substring (index 5, length 10)
        //    XMLStreamWriter.writeCharacters(char[], int, int)
        //    automatically escapes XML‑special characters.
        xmlWriter.writeCharacters(textBuffer, 5, 10); // writes 10 chars starting at index 5

        // 6. Close the elements and the document
        xmlWriter.writeEndElement();                 //   </content>
        xmlWriter.writeEndElement();                 // </root>
        xmlWriter.writeEndDocument();

        // 7. Flush & close the writer
        xmlWriter.flush();
        xmlWriter.close();

        // 8. Inspect the resulting XML
        System.out.println("Generated XML:");
        System.out.println(stringWriter.toString());
    }
}
```

### What the code does

| Step | What happens | Why it matters |
|------|--------------|----------------|
| 1 | `textBuffer` holds your raw characters. | You can use any source – a file, network, etc. |
| 2 | `StringWriter` is just a convenient in‑memory `Writer`. | Replace it with `FileWriter`, `OutputStreamWriter`, etc., if you need to write to disk or a network socket. |
| 3 | `XMLStreamWriter` is the StAX API that writes XML. | It automatically escapes `<`, `>`, `&`, `"`, `'` when you call `writeCharacters`. |
| 4 | Start the XML document and open a `<root>` element. | Gives context for the text you’re about to write. |
| 5 | `writeCharacters(textBuffer, 5, 10)` writes exactly 10 characters starting at index 5. | This is the “append a substring” part. |
| 6 | Close the tags and finish the document. | Properly nested XML is required for a well‑formed document. |
| 7 | Flush & close the writer. | Ensures all data is actually written out. |
| 8 | Print the XML to the console. | Shows the result; you can also write it to a file. |

### Result

Running the program prints something like:

```
Generated XML:
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?><root><content>o, this i</content></root>
```

Notice that the substring `"o, this i"` (10 characters starting at index 5) is correctly inserted inside `<content>`. If any of those 10 characters were XML‑special (e.g., `<` or `&`), the serializer would have escaped them automatically.

---

## If you need to **manually escape** before writing

In rare cases you might want to escape the characters yourself (e.g., if you’re not using StAX). Apache Commons Text provides a handy utility:

```java
import org.apache.commons.text.StringEscapeUtils;

// ...
String escaped = StringEscapeUtils.escapeXml10(new String(textBuffer, 5, 10));
xmlWriter.writeCharacters(escaped);
```

But with `XMLStreamWriter.writeCharacters` you normally don’t need this step.

---

## Using JAXB (if you’re marshalling objects)

If you’re already using JAXB to marshal Java objects to XML, you can still inject raw text by adding a `@XmlValue` field or by using a `@XmlElement` that contains a `String`. JAXB will escape the string for you. For example:

```java
@XmlRootElement
public class Content {
    @XmlValue
    private String text;

    // getters/setters
}
```

Then set `content.setText(new String(textBuffer, 5, 10));` and marshal the `Content` object.

---

### TL;DR

* Use `XMLStreamWriter.writeCharacters(char[] ch, int start, int length)` to append a substring of a `char[]` to an XML stream.
* The method automatically escapes XML‑special characters.
* Wrap the writer in a `StringWriter` (or any `Writer`) to capture the output, or write directly to a file/stream.
* If you’re using JAXB, set a `String` field and let JAXB handle the escaping.

Happy XML‑coding!

